# ultrahdr-core gain-map SIMD vs scalar on aarch64 — SIMD LOSES
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
command: cargo bench --package ultrahdr-core --bench simd_xplat --features simd
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native

The bench already existed and already compares scalar against SIMD; it had
simply never been recorded on ARM.

## Result — the SIMD variants are SLOWER

| variant     | 512x512   | 1920x1080 | vs scalar     |
|-------------|-----------|-----------|---------------|
| scalar_lut  |  94.06us  |  750.93us | (baseline)    |
| simd_lut    | 168.40us  |  1.3319ms | 1.78x SLOWER  |
| simd_exp    | 356.31us  |  2.7926ms | 3.75x SLOWER  |

Consistent at both sizes, low variance. This is not noise.

## Cause

`apply_gain_row_scalar` does one table load per pixel: `lut[gainmap[i]]`.
AArch64 NEON has **no gather instruction**, so the vectorised form must perform
eight scalar loads plus lane-inserts to assemble each f32x8 of gains — strictly
more work than doing the multiply scalar. LUT-indexed kernels are the standard
case where SIMD loses on NEON.

`simd_exp` skips the LUT and evaluates the gain with transcendentals instead,
which is worse again (3.75x) — a 256-entry LUT is very hard to beat.

## Production impact: NONE

`apply_gainmap` does not call these. It calls `apply_gain_row_presampled`,
which — despite living in `apply_simd.rs` — is a plain scalar loop
(apply_simd.rs:40). So the crate's own gain-map application is unaffected.

The exposure is the PUBLIC API: `apply_gain_row_simd` is `pub`, its doc said
only that aarch64 "processes 8 pixels per iteration", and a caller picking it
for speed on ARM would get a ~1.8x slowdown. That doc now carries the measured
table and points at `apply_gain_row_scalar`.

## Not done

The kernel is not rewritten. Making it competitive on NEON would mean dropping
the LUT for a computed gain (measured worse — that is `simd_exp`), or a
vectorised table lookup via `vqtbl4q_u8`, which handles only a 64-entry table
per instruction and would need four of them plus range selects for 256 entries.
Neither is obviously a win, and it would change x86 codegen too, which cannot
be verified on this aarch64-only host. Documented rather than guessed at.
